# =============================================================================
# RAGNART .ragignore template (config/ragignore.example)
# =============================================================================
# TWO FILES, TWO ROLES
#   config/ragignore is the LIVE global exclusion list. It applies to every NAS
#   root on every scan, with the lowest precedence. It is versioned with the
#   code, like git's core.excludesFile, and holds system-wide rules such as
#   .git/, node_modules/ and .venv/.
#   This file is an INERT template. Everything in it is commented out, so it
#   excludes nothing. Copy it into a NAS directory as `.ragignore` for that
#   directory's own rules. Do not copy config/ragignore as a template: that
#   would duplicate the global rules.
#
# WHAT THIS IS
#   A template. It lists every option a .ragignore file accepts, with a short
#   explanation and an example for each. Every example is commented out, so
#   using this file as it is changes nothing.
#
#   To use it, copy it into any directory on the NAS as `.ragignore`, then
#   activate only the examples you need. Each example line starts with the
#   marker "#> ". To activate one, delete the marker and keep the rest of the
#   line exactly as written, trailing spaces included.
#
# HOW .ragignore FILES WORK
#   The syntax and matching are those of gitignore, exactly. `git check-ignore`
#   is the reference. See `docs/RAGNART-design.md` §1.2.
#   - Patterns are relative to the directory that holds the .ragignore file.
#   - A .ragignore in a deeper directory takes precedence over one in a
#     shallower directory.
#   - config/ragignore, the global file, has the lowest precedence. Its
#     patterns are relative to the crawled root.
#   - Within one file, the last matching pattern wins.
#   - A path inside an excluded directory cannot be re-included.
#   - These rules apply to the NAS crawl only. The Claude Code and OpenCode
#     session scanners do not read them.
#   - .gitignore files are not read.
#   - An unreadable .ragignore stops the sync. The sync exits with an error and
#     runs no deletions.
#
# EFFECT ON THE INDEX
#   A newly ignored item leaves the index at the next sync, subject to the
#   deletion guards. The sync log names the .ragignore file and line that
#   matched (`docs/RAGNART-design.md` §1.2, deviation A).
#
# CHECKING PATHS
#   ragnart check-ignore -v <path>   shows the file, line and pattern that decide <path>
#   ragnart ls-files --ignored       lists the on-disk files that the rules exclude

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Blank lines and comments
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Blank lines are ignored, and so is any line that starts with '#'.
#> # A comment line. This example is also ignored.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Escapes: \# and \!
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A backslash makes the next character literal. Use it for a name that starts
# with '#', which would otherwise be a comment, or with '!', which would
# otherwise re-include.
#> \#draft.md
#> \!bang.txt

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Trailing spaces
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Unescaped trailing spaces are stripped, so "name   " means "name". To keep one,
# escape it with a backslash: "name\ " matches a file called "name ".
#> scratch\ 

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Wildcards: * and ?
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# '*' matches any run of characters, except '/'. '?' matches one character,
# except '/'.
#> *.tmp
#> draft-?.md

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Bracket expressions: [abc], [a-z], [!abc]
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# '[abc]' matches one of a, b or c. '[a-z]' matches one character in the range.
# '[!abc]' matches one character that is not a, b or c.
#> scan-[abc].pdf
#> photo-[a-z].jpg
#> draft-[!abc].md

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# POSIX character classes: [[:digit:]]
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A class goes inside a bracket. The names are alnum, alpha, blank, cntrl,
# digit, graph, lower, print, punct, space, upper and xdigit. Git uses the C
# locale, so only ASCII characters match.
#> part-[[:digit:]].bin

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# No slash: matches at any depth
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A pattern with no '/' except a trailing one matches at any depth below this
# directory, in files and in directories.
#> Thumbs.db

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leading slash: anchored to this directory
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A leading '/' anchors the pattern to the directory that holds this file.
#> /TODO.md

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Middle slash: also anchored to this directory
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A '/' anywhere except at the end anchors the pattern, so this matches only
# docs/draft.md, not sub/docs/draft.md.
#> docs/draft.md

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Trailing slash: directories only
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A trailing '/' matches directories only. A file with the same name is not
# matched.
#> cache/

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leading **/: any depth
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# '**/' at the start matches in any directory, at any depth.
#> **/private/

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Trailing /**: everything inside
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 'x/**' matches everything inside x, but not x itself.
#> archive/**

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Middle /**/: any number of directories
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 'a/**/b' matches b in a, in any sub-directory of a, at any depth, including
# directly in a.
#> docs/**/internal.md

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Negation: ! re-includes
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A leading '!' re-includes a path that an earlier pattern in the same file
# excluded. The last matching pattern decides. The pair below excludes every
# .log file except keep.log, at any depth.
#> *.log
#> !keep.log

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Re-including inside an excluded directory: the pitfall
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A path inside an excluded directory cannot be re-included, because the walk
# never enters that directory. Writing "dir/" and then "!dir/keep.md" does
# nothing: dir/keep.md stays excluded.
#
# Exclude the contents instead. "dir/*" excludes what is directly in dir/, so
# dir/ itself is still entered, and "!dir/keep.md" re-includes keep.md.
# Subdirectories of dir/ are excluded with their contents.
#> dir/*
#> !dir/keep.md

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-file opt-out by exact name
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A .ragignore in a deeper directory can re-include one file by its exact name,
# when a shallower .ragignore or config/ragignore excludes it. The deeper file
# has higher precedence, so its line wins for that file only.
#> !README.md

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Typical use: build output
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compiled output that a build recreates on each run.
#> build/
#> target/

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Typical use: generated reports
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Output of a script rather than authored content.
#> generated-reports/

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Typical use: a large data folder
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Bulk exports that would dominate the index.
#> dumps/

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Typical use: log files
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Log files at any depth.
#> *.log
